// CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Analysis of failures:
// 1. 6 examples: no << >> emitted (model reasons forever without opening a span)
// 2. 3 examples: malformed content inside span ({var} syntax, ** operator)
// 3. 23 examples: syntax_valid_wrong (correct structure, wrong semantic content)
// 4. The model generates very long unconstrained reasoning before (or instead of) opening spans
//
// Root causes:
// A) The model often writes the final answer formula OUTSIDE << >> delimiters, never opening a span
// B) When the model does open a span, it sometimes inserts {var} template syntax which the parser rejects
// C) The unconstrained reasoning is too long (avg 132 tokens before first <<)
//
// Key insight from diagnostics:
// - "Constrained intervention activity": only 8/49 examples had any constrained activity
// - Most spans (35/49) were generated entirely by unconstrained steps (model naturally emitted <<...>>)
// - The constrained decoder was barely used, meaning the model's free generation already handles most cases
// - The 6 no-span failures hit max steps (token_budget_band: high, gen_time_band: slow)
//   → these are cases where the model just keeps reasoning and never converges to a final answer
//
// Strategy: Use GenerateWithPrefixAndManagedSpan with a short prefix budget
// This forces entry into a constrained span after at most prefixBudget free steps,
// ensuring we ALWAYS get a << >> span even for verbose reasoners.
// The managed span handles all span lifecycle automatically.
//
// The prefixBudget controls how long we allow unconstrained reasoning before forcing a span.
// Setting it to ~500 allows sufficient reasoning while guaranteeing span entry.
// After the span opens, AdaptiveConstrainedStep (used internally) handles valid token generation.
//
// For the malformed content issue ({var} syntax): the parser hard-constrains tokens inside the span,
// so {, }, ** cannot appear if the parser doesn't allow them. This eliminates malformed spans.
//
// For no-span failures: prefixBudget ensures we force open a span before running out of budget.
//
